{
  "concept": "A position vector v = ai + bj is the head-to-tail sum of its horizontal and vertical component vectors, and those components form the legs of a right triangle whose hypotenuse length is |v| = sqrt(a² + b²).",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Thin black horizontal axis with arrowheads at both ends, a few short unlabeled tick marks",
    "Thin black vertical axis with arrowheads at both ends, a few short unlabeled tick marks",
    "Origin point where the axes cross",
    "Orange arrow from origin into the first quadrant representing the vector v",
    "Bold italic label 'v = ai + bj' placed in the upper-left region near the orange arrow",
    "Dark blue horizontal arrow from origin along the x-axis to (a, 0), the component ai",
    "Label 'ai' (bold i) below the horizontal blue arrow",
    "Dark blue vertical arrow from (a, 0) up to (a, b), the component bj",
    "Label 'bj' (bold j) to the right of the vertical blue arrow",
    "Right-angle square marker at (a, 0) between the two blue arrows (shown in triangle view)",
    "Dashed light-gray guide lines from the vector tip to each axis (magnitude view)",
    "Numeric side-length labels 'a', 'b' and hypotenuse label '√(a² + b²)' shown in the magnitude view",
    "Readout panel showing current a, b, and computed |v|",
    "Legend keying orange = resultant vector v, dark blue = component vectors"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "aComponent",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "a (horizontal component)",
      "range": [-6, 6, 0.5],
      "default": 4,
      "teaches": "The ai arrow stretches or flips along the x-axis and drags the tip of v sideways, isolating the purely horizontal share of the vector."
    },
    {
      "id": "bComponent",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "b (vertical component)",
      "range": [-6, 6, 0.5],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "The bj arrow grows or shrinks vertically from the end of ai, moving the tip of v up or down and isolating the purely vertical share."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Components", "Magnitude triangle"],
      "default": "Components",
      "teaches": "The same picture read two ways: as a head-to-tail sum of component vectors, or as a right triangle whose legs |a| and |b| give |v| = sqrt(a² + b²)."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The vector and its parts",
      "narration": "An orange arrow v reaches into the first quadrant. Two dark blue arrows, ai along the x-axis and bj rising from its tip, are joined head to tail and land exactly on v's tip.",
      "state": { "aComponent": 4, "bComponent": 3, "viewMode": "Components" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Stretch the horizontal part",
      "narration": "Increasing a lengthens only the ai arrow; the tip of v slides right and the vector tilts flatter, while bj is untouched.",
      "state": { "aComponent": 6, "bComponent": 3, "viewMode": "Components" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Stretch the vertical part",
      "narration": "Now raise b. Only bj grows, the tip climbs, and v steepens — the two components act completely independently.",
      "state": { "aComponent": 6, "bComponent": 6, "viewMode": "Components" }
    },
    {
      "title": "A negative component flips direction",
      "narration": "Set a negative and the ai arrow points left, carrying v into the second quadrant. The sum rule still holds: v always ends where bj ends.",
      "state": { "aComponent": -3, "bComponent": 5, "viewMode": "Components" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read off the magnitude",
      "narration": "Switch to the triangle view: the components become labeled legs with a right angle between them, and the hypotenuse is |v| = sqrt(a² + b²).",
      "state": { "aComponent": 4, "bComponent": 3, "viewMode": "Magnitude triangle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch the hypotenuse respond",
      "narration": "Change either leg and the hypotenuse updates by the Pythagorean theorem — length depends on both components, but never on their signs.",
      "state": { "aComponent": -4, "bComponent": 3, "viewMode": "Magnitude triangle" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "ai and bj are not just labels on a pair of numbers — they are actual arrows laid head to tail, so the vector IS their sum and its length is automatically the hypotenuse of the right triangle they form.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Center the origin in the plotting area with a plain unlabeled cross of thin black axes, arrowheads at all four ends, and a handful of small evenly spaced tick marks on each. Draw the resultant v as a medium-weight orange arrow from the origin to (a, b); draw ai as a dark navy arrow from the origin along the x-axis to (a, 0) and bj as a dark navy arrow from (a, 0) to (a, b), each with a solid arrowhead. Label 'v = ai + bj' in bold serif math near the interior upper-left of the vector, 'ai' just below the horizontal arrow, 'bj' just right of the vertical arrow. In the magnitude-triangle view, keep the same geometry but lighten the component arrows to plain line segments, add a small right-angle square at (a, 0), replace the arrow labels with numeric leg lengths, and annotate the orange hypotenuse with '|v| = √(a² + b²)' plus its computed value. Keep the drawing region symmetric so negative a or b still fits on screen; a small readout in a corner shows the live a, b, and |v|.",
  "figureCaption": "CNX_Precalc_Figure_08_08_010 — a position vector resolved into horizontal and vertical component vectors."
}

